{
  "stage": "write",
  "item": "ch03.s11",
  "inputs_hash": "25e9b46e6226a65310cfbbc7edaa5d5a09b19d8f715bfa79b8eebdfffdd7bd4b",
  "output_hash": "9b802c07d0ec6885643e78d01c3d65af2efe68ff0adb4e4ee980b36efd19a0a0",
  "output": {
    "section_id": "ch03.s11",
    "paragraphs": [
      {
        "id": "ch03.s11.p01",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p01.01",
            "text": "Why do transformer blocks include those extra arrows and plus signs, and why do they matter for training stability?",
            "type": "C",
            "cites": []
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p01.02",
            "text": "Shortcut connections are an important building block of the GPT architecture alongside layer normalization, the GELU activation, and the feed-forward neural network.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@23",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@28",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@30",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@32",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@35",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1775",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1784"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p01.03",
            "text": "In a Transformer block, the plus symbols and their associated arrows represent shortcut connections.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@117",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@122",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@124",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@130",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@132",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1877",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1879"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p01.04",
            "text": "Shortcut connections are also known as skip connections or residual connections.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@157",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@158",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@160",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@162",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@164"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p01.05",
            "text": "They were first proposed in computer vision to solve the vanishing gradient problem.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@167",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@172",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@174",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@178"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ch03.s11.p02",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p02.01",
            "text": "The vanishing gradient problem is that gradients become progressively smaller as they are propagated backward through a neural network.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@194",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@195",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@198",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@200"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p02.02",
            "text": "During backpropagation, gradient flow goes from the output of the network to the input, so later-layer gradients are used to compute earlier-layer gradients.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@252",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@254",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@256",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@258",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@265",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@267",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@269",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@271",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@273"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p02.03",
            "text": "Because backpropagation involves multiplications, if a later gradient becomes small then earlier gradients become even smaller, which is why gradients can vanish.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@273",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@275",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@277",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@279",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@282",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@286",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@288",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@290",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@292",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@295",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@299",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@302",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@304",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@306"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p02.04",
            "text": "When gradients become very small, weight updates are not made, learning becomes stagnant, convergence is delayed, and the language model does not learn well.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@200",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@202",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@205",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@207",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@210",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@212"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p02.05",
            "text": "The weight update rule is that the new weight equals the old weight minus the step size times the partial derivative of the loss with respect to the weight.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@324",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@327",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@329",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@331",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@333",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@336",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@339"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p02.06",
            "text": "If the gradient of the loss with respect to a weight approaches zero, the weight is not updated, so the neural network is not learning.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@349",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@351",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@353",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@355",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@357",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@362",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@364",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@366",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@368",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@370",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@373",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@375",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@376"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p02.07",
            "text": "This leads to stagnation, and shortcut connections solve that problem.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@378",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@381",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@385",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@388",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@391",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@393"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ch03.s11.p03",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p03.01",
            "text": "A shortcut connection creates an alternative path for the gradient to flow by skipping one or more layers.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@398",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@401",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@403",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@407"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p03.02",
            "text": "It is implemented by adding the output of one layer to the output of a later layer.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@412",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@415",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@512",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@515"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p03.03",
            "text": "For two layers, if the earlier output is y_l and the later layer computes f(y_l), then with a shortcut connection the later output becomes y_(l+1) = f(y_l) + y_l.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@602",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@606",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@610",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@620",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@622",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@624",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@626",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@629",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@630",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@658",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@662",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@672",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@673",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@677",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@679"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p03.04",
            "text": "To prevent vanishing gradients, the partial derivative of the loss with respect to the first-layer output should stay as large as possible so that the gradients with respect to the first-layer weights do not go to zero.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@687",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@688",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@701",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@703",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@705",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@707",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@709",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@711",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@713",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@715",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@717"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p03.05",
            "text": "Using the chain rule, the partial derivative of the loss with respect to y_l equals the partial derivative of the loss with respect to y_(l+1) multiplied by the partial derivative of y_(l+1) with respect to y_l.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@724",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@725",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@728",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@729",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@732",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@734"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p03.06",
            "text": "Because y_(l+1) = f(y_l) + y_l, the derivative of y_(l+1) with respect to y_l includes the derivative of f(y_l) plus 1.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@739",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@741",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@743",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@746",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@748"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p03.07",
            "text": "That plus-one term keeps the gradient from vanishing even if the derivative through the main path becomes small.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@767",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@770",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@776",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@780",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@785",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@788",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@790",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@793",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@795",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@799",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@800",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@802",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@804",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@806"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p03.08",
            "text": "Because the shortcut connection keeps gradients flowing, the loss gradients with respect to earlier-layer weights do not ultimately vanish, so training does not stagnate.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@809",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@811",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@813",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@815",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@818",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@820",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@822",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@824",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@826",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@828",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@830",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@832",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@835",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@839",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@841",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@843",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@845",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@847"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p03.09",
            "text": "Shortcut connections therefore play a crucial role during backpropagation by preserving gradient flow through the network.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@878",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@879",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@882",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@884",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@887"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p03.10",
            "text": "They also smooth the loss-function landscape and make gradient flow smoother.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@925",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@927",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@928",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@931",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@946",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@948",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@949",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@952"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ch03.s11.p04",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p04.01",
            "text": "A small code example makes this effect concrete by stacking multiple layers, optionally adding shortcut connections, and then comparing gradient-flow magnitudes with and without shortcuts.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@966",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@967",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@969",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@976",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@979",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@981",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@984",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@991",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@993",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@996",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@998",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1000"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p04.02",
            "text": "The argument \"layer_sizes\" specifies how many neurons are in each layer.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1016",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1018",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1021"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p04.03",
            "text": "If \"layer_sizes\" is \"3, 3, 3, 3, 1\", the network has five layers with three neurons and a final layer with one neuron.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1023",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1026",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1029",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1030",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1033",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1036",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1038",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1040"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p04.04",
            "text": "The argument \"use_shortcut\" determines whether shortcut connections are used: \"false\" disables them and \"true\" enables them.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1047",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1049",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1051",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1053",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1055"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p04.05",
            "text": "PyTorch's \"nn.Sequential\" module connects different neural network layers together.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1057",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1059",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1064",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1066",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1069",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1070"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p04.06",
            "text": "The network is constructed by chaining layers so that one entry of \"layer_sizes\" gives the input dimension, the next entry gives the output dimension, and each layer uses a GELU activation function.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1080",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1083",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1084",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1087",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1088",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1091",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1094",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1096",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1097",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1099",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1236",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1238"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p04.07",
            "text": "For a layer, the input dimension is determined by the previous layer's output size and the output dimension is determined by the next layer's size.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1145",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1146",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1148",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1150",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1152",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1194",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1198",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1200",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1202",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1206",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1207",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1210",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1212",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1214",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1217",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1220",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1222"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p04.08",
            "text": "The code uses the GELU activation function.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1088",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1099",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1236",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1238"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ch03.s11.p05",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p05.01",
            "text": "The forward pass differs only in whether shortcut connections are added.",
            "type": "C",
            "cites": []
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p05.02",
            "text": "Without shortcut connections, the output of each layer is fed to the next layer until the final output is produced.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1250",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1253",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1255",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1259",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1261",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1263",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1265",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1267",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1269",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1271"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p05.03",
            "text": "With shortcut connections, each step adds the current layer output to the running value from previous steps, so the final result accumulates all earlier shortcut additions.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1274",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1276",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1280",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1282",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1286",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1289",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1292",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1294",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1296",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1302",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1304",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1308",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1310",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1317",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1319",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1321",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1325",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1327",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1331",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1333",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1335",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1337",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1341",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1344"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p05.04",
            "text": "The shortcut connection itself is implemented by a single line that updates \"x\" to \"x\" plus the layer output.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1349",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1350"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p05.05",
            "text": "The implemented deep neural network has five layers, each consisting of a linear layer and a GELU activation, and the forward pass optionally adds shortcut connections when the \"use_shortcut\" attribute is true.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1354",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1355",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1358",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1360",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1363",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1365",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1366",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1368",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1371",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1372"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ch03.s11.p06",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p06.01",
            "text": "To inspect gradient flow, the example first builds a model without shortcut connections.",
            "type": "C",
            "cites": []
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p06.02",
            "text": "The example initializes a network without shortcut connections using layer sizes \"3, 3, 3, 3, 1\" and a three-dimensional sample input \"[1, 0, -1]\".",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1375",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1377",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1383",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1385",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1388",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1390",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1393",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1417",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1421",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1423",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1426",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1428"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p06.03",
            "text": "In layers with input dimension 3 and output dimension 3, each neuron has three weights, so the weight matrix is 3 by 3, while the final layer has a 3 by 1 weight matrix.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1457",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1458",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1460",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1462",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1467",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1470",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1472",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1474",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1476",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1478",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1480",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1482"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p06.04",
            "text": "To summarize gradient flow per layer, the backward pass computes gradients for all weights in a layer and then takes the mean of those values as that layer's mean gradient.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1482",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1484",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1487",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1489",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1491",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1494",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1495",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1498",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1500",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1501",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1503",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1506",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1508",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1510",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1513",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1515"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p06.05",
            "text": "The loss is defined as the squared difference between the model output \"y\" and the ground truth, for example \"(y - 0)\" squared when the ground truth is zero.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1527",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1530",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1532",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1534",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1538"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p06.06",
            "text": "In backpropagation, the partial derivatives of the loss are computed starting from the output layer and then moving backward through earlier layers until the first layer.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1549",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1551",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1552",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1554",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1556",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1557",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1559",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1561",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1562",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1565"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p06.07",
            "text": "In PyTorch, the \"backward\" method conveniently computes loss gradients during the backward pass.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1640",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1642",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1644",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1646"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p06.08",
            "text": "The code sets the target to zero, computes the model output for \"x\", uses a squared loss, calls \"loss.backward()\", and then prints a single gradient value per layer by taking the mean of each layer's gradient matrix.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1566",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1568",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1570",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1573",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1574",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1576",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1578",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1580",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1584",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1587",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1589",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1630",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1633",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1635",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1637"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ch03.s11.p07",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p07.01",
            "text": "The printed gradients show the difference clearly.",
            "type": "C",
            "cites": []
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p07.02",
            "text": "Without shortcut connections, the mean gradient becomes much smaller in earlier layers, illustrating the vanishing gradient problem.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1658",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1659",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1662",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1665",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1668",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1670",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1672",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1675",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1676",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1679",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1681",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1683",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1685"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p07.03",
            "text": "Adding shortcut connections creates alternative paths for gradients to flow between layers.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1698",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1700",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1702",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1705",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1707",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1709",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1711",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1713",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1715",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1718",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1881",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1883"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p07.04",
            "text": "With shortcut connections, the earlier-layer gradients remain much larger and do not become negligible, showing that shortcut connections solve the vanishing gradient problem in this example.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1722",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1724",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1726",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1729",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1730",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1733",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1736",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1738",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1740",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1743",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1745",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1748",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1751",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1753",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1755"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p07.05",
            "text": "With shortcut connections, the gradient values stabilize toward the first layer instead of shrinking to a vanishingly small value.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1757",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1759",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1761",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1763"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p07.06",
            "text": "Shortcut connections are an important way to overcome the vanishing gradient problem in deep neural networks.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1775",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1777",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1779",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1781"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p07.07",
            "text": "They are a core building block of large language models and help training by ensuring consistent gradient flow across layers in GPT models.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1784",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1786",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1788",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1791",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1793"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p07.08",
            "text": "They are used because they help stabilize training by stabilizing gradient flow and solving the vanishing gradient problem.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1833",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1835",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1836",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1839",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1841"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ch03.s11.p08",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p08.01",
            "text": "Returning to the Transformer block, the reason for those arrows is now clear.",
            "type": "C",
            "cites": []
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p08.02",
            "text": "The arrows between layers are shortcut connections, and they provide an alternative path for gradients to flow.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1872",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1875",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1877",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1879",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1881",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1883"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p08.03",
            "text": "A shortcut connection can be added between layers of the Transformer block to help solve the vanishing gradient problem.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1885",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1886",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1888",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1890"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s11.p08.04",
            "text": "Because backpropagation passes through the entire Transformer block from output to input, the architecture must ensure that gradients do not vanish so that learning remains stable.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1893",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1895",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1896",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1900",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1902",
              "2r0QahNdwMw@1905"
            ]
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
